  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Gender equality</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">BRAC&#x2019;s Social Empowerment and Legal Protection (SELP) program provides legal, medical, accommodation and counselling services to women survivors of violence. It is also works to increase understanding of sexual and reproductive health in adolescents, marginalised and gender diverse populations in Bangladesh. Through local governments and village community organisations, the SELP is raising awareness to end child marriage, harmful dowry practices and domestic violence. The program also works with men and boys to promote equal decision making and to change attitudes surrounding gender-based violence. In 2022, through BRAC, Australia assisted 11,040 survivors of gender-based violence to access services.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Gender equality</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">BRAC&#x2019;s Social Empowerment and Legal Protection SELP aimed to contribute to the reduction of gender-based violence and harmful practices in 31 districts of Bangladesh with the highest number of GBV cases by focusing on reducing child marriage and strengthening the legal protection system. While the focus on legal protection for violence survivors remained the same, the community mobilisation effort was further streamlined to a household-based approach addressing gender-based violence. The program also continued its effort to improve access to quality legal protection services for survivors of violence against women and girls (VAWG) through raising awareness, enhancing policy implementation, reviewing and driving for policy reforms and bolstering strategic engagement with local, national and regional platforms dedicated to combating child marriage and VAWG.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Education</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">In 2022, Australia assisted 51,439 disadvantaged girls and boys to receive a quality education through the BRAC Education Program (BEP). Addressing learning losses caused by the long shut down of schools during Covid-19 remains a key focus for the BEP. The program includes an accelerated model for children who have dropped out of school, teaching &#x201c;must know&#x201d; competencies to cover a two-year syllabus in 10 months. Remedial plans, diverse supplementary materials, and detailed teachers&#x2019; guides were updated this year to incorporate lessons learned and new research.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Economic empowerment</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The UPG program adopts a graduation approach which is a holistic, climate-sensitive, context-specific, and time-bound &amp; sequenced set of interventions targeting women participants to enable their extremely poor households to progress along a pathway to a sustainable livelihood, reduced inequality, and socioeconomic resilience. The UPG program provides a platform for marginalised women to attain financial security and economic empowerment and provides cash/productive assets along with livelihood options. Women participants can access important life skills, knowledge on social matters, and government services. The program also provides training &amp; other support to women to engage in trades which have traditionally been male dominated. It works to sustainably lift ultra-poor households from poverty by improving women&#x2019;s earning potential, keeping them at the centre of economic activities and helping them gain a higher level of empowerment.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Social protection measures</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The UPG program adopts a graduation approach which is a holistic, climate-sensitive, context-specific, and time-bound &amp; sequenced set of interventions, that aims to enable extremely poor households to progress along a pathway to a sustainable livelihood, reduced inequality and socioeconomic resilience. The program addresses multidimensional poverty, addressing context-specific and people-specific challenges. It has interventions in rural and urban settings that are climate-adaptive and targeted for populations with diversified needs and vulnerabilities, such as persons with disabilities, minority communities, and host communities living in a humanitarian context. The program works to sustainably lift ultra-poor households from poverty.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Economic empowerment</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The UPG program provides a platform for marginalised women to attain financial security and economic empowerment. In addition to acquiring a productive asset, the participants receive financial literacy and business management training. In 2022, some participants went on to run small businesses in homestead gardening, hydroponics, aquaponics, plant cultivation and sesame production. 
&#x200d;
Through the UPG program marginalised women are gaining greater access to resources, decision making and economic independence. In 2022, Australia helped 29,341 women to build sustainable pathways out of poverty by becoming small business entrepreneurs.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Social protection measures</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">To promote sustainable poverty reduction, BRAC&#x2019;s Ultra-Poor Graduation (UPG) program implements inclusive, climate-resilient, and context-specific strategies tailored to the needs of marginalized households and communities. The program follows the Graduation Approach&#x2014;a holistic, time-bound, and sequenced set of interventions designed to help people living in extreme poverty build sustainable livelihoods, enhance resilience, and reduce inequality. In 2024, BRAC expanded its efforts by targeting climate-vulnerable populations through integrated, multi-sectoral support under both the UPG and Integrated Development Program (IDP). These initiatives provided a combination of asset transfers, skills training, financial inclusion, and social empowerment, with a strong focus on gender and disability inclusion. Programmatic insights highlighted that combining the Graduation Approach with employment creation and skills development significantly boosts income mobility and job opportunities&#x2014;especially for women and persons with disabilities. This integrated model strengthens long-term socioeconomic resilience and supports inclusive development outcomes.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Social protection measures</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">With Australian assistance, the UPG program supports marginalised, ultra-poor women by providing them with a regular stipend, a one-off grant of a productive asset, training on how to look after and grow that asset, and access to free health care. In 2022, the program piloted a disability-inclusive model, and this version of the program is currently being introduced. The UPG is also increasing its focus on building resilience to   shocks and on climate-adaptive livelihoods, in addition to the usual support for basic needs. Through completion of this program, participants graduate&#x2019; sustainably from ultra-poverty.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
